A small IP stack for RSTS/E V10.1 that runs as an ordinary detached job,
TCPIPD, on the RSTS/E Ethernet portal interface (XE:, DEUNA/DELUA).

  - TCP: connections over pseudo keyboards (a program reads and writes
    KBn:), 4 KB receive window, out-of-order segments kept and reported
    with SACK, several segments in flight, retransmission and fast
    retransmit
  - UDP for programs; DNS resolver (name servers from DHCP, or DNS and
    DOMAIN in TCPIP.INI)
  - ARP, ICMP echo, CIDR routing, DHCP client
  - Commands:
      PING host [count]
      HOST name ...            addresses of names; HOST alone: settings
      IFCONFIG                 the interface: MAC, addresses, DHCP lease,
                               packets and bytes in and out, errors
      TLOAD [folder/]name      MiSTer tape library: put NAME.TAP or
                               NAME.TPC from games/PDP2011 (or a folder
                               of it, RSTS/V9.6/NAME.TAP; a name alone is
                               found in any folder) on MT0:, then
                               MOUNT MT0: as usual. TLOAD alone: what is
                               on MT0:; TLOAD /UNLOAD empties the drive.
                               A tape in use is not swapped (DISMOUNT
                               first). Needs MiSTer_pdp2011 with tape
                               library support, Host network not Off.
      TDIR [folder]            list the tape library: its folders
                               (NAME/) and tapes; a folder; or
      TDIR [folder/]pattern    matching names (* wildcard)
      NETDATE [/SET] [/QUICK] [server]   the time from a network time
                               server (SNTP): shows it and how far off the
                               system is; /SET sets the date and time, to
                               about half a second (it waits for the next
                               minute, up to a minute); /QUICK sets it at
                               once, to the nearest minute. Server: NTP
                               in TCPIP.INI, else the gateway. Local time
                               from TIMEZONE in
                               TCPIP.INI (a POSIX TZ string, e.g.
                               PST8PDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0 or CET-1CEST,M3.5.0,
                               M10.5.0/3); TCPIP.INS runs NETDATE /SET at
                               start-up when TIMEZONE is given. Add the
                               NTP and TIMEZONE lines to an existing
                               TCPIP.INI by hand (installation keeps it).
                               RSTS keeps time by its line clock: INIT
                               HARDWR HERTZ must match the clock (MiSTer
                               OSD 50/60 Hz), or the time drifts by a
                               sixth however often NETDATE sets it.
      NC[/INPUT=file][/OUTPUT=file][/TEXT|/BINARY] host port
      NC[/INPUT=file][/OUTPUT=file][/TEXT|/BINARY]/LISTEN=port
                               a TCP connection on your terminal (or wait
                               for one); /INPUT sends a file, /OUTPUT
                               saves what arrives (text or binary as for
                               CURL) - RSTS has no pipes. Unix-style -i,
                               -o, -t, -b, -L work too.
      CURL[/OUTPUT=file][/HEADERS|/HEAD][/FOLLOW][/QUIET][/VERBOSE]
          [/INSECURE][/TEXT|/BINARY][/PROXY=host:port] url
                               fetch http:// URLs; https:// through a proxy
                               that does TLS (PROXY host:port in TCPIP.INI).
                               Unix-style -o, -i, -h, -L, -s, -v, -k, -t,
                               -b, -x work too.
  - TFTP server, now its own job TFTPD (TFTP NO turns it off)
  - Files received (CURL -o, TFTP put): text (a text/... response, CURL -t,
    TFTP netascii) becomes an ordinary RSTS text file - CR LF lines, no
    attributes - that editors and compilers read; anything else is binary,
    stored with RMS attributes of undefined record format and its exact
    end (end-of-file block and first free byte). Files are sent (TFTP get,
    NC -S) up to that recorded end
  - Command lines: every command reads its whole line (127 characters)
    and takes RSTS-style qualifiers (TLOAD/UNLOAD, NETDATE/SET/QUICK) as
    well as ones after a space.
  - For C programmers: BSD-style sockets (SOCK.H; socket, bind, listen,
    accept, connect, send, recv, sendto, recvfrom, gethostbyname, ...),
    and CMDLIN.H (cmdarg: the whole command line, split RSTS style).
    Link with  LINK/CC prog,TCPIP$:CMDLIN,TCPIP$:RESOLV,TCPIP$:SOCK,
    TCPIP$:ETHIO  (compile with the headers from TCPIP$:).

Monitor fix (XEPATCH, default YES): the RSTS/E V10.1 DEUNA/DELUA driver
crashes the system when it receives a frame of 629-632 bytes (the frame's
CRC alone lands in the last of its 632-byte receive buffers and the driver
copies a negative length). Real DEUNAs and SIMH both produce such frames.
XEPAT also fixes the monitor's set-time call (UU.DAT): it zeroed the
clock's tick counter, so the first second after the time was set lasted
over 4 seconds (Paul Koning's timfix, found by its code pattern). That fix
is in an overlay and works at once; it is needed for NETDATE.
XEPAT reads this system's own monitor, checks the exact code, and writes an
ONLPAT command file that makes the driver skip that copy; ONLPAT verifies
every old word before it changes anything, so a monitor that differs is
left alone. The fix goes into the monitor file and takes effect at the next
boot. TCPIP.INS repeats the check at every start, so a monitor you build
later gets the fix too; TCP/IP then waits for the reboot. XEPATCH NO turns
this off (TCPIP$:XEPAT.NO).

HTTPS: tools/sslproxy.py from the distribution runs on a nearby machine
(e.g. the MiSTer's Linux) and fetches https:// URLs for CURL, checking the
certificate; CURL shows what it reported (-v) and refuses an untrusted
certificate unless -k is given.

Files (TCPIP$: = SY:[0,91]):
  TCPIPD.TSK  the TCP/IP job        TFTPD.TSK  the TFTP server
  PING.TSK NC.TSK HOST.TSK CURL.TSK IFCONF.TSK TLOAD.TSK NETDAT.TSK
              commands (TLOAD.TSK is both TLOAD and TDIR)
  XEPAT.TSK   the monitor fix check TCPIP.INI  settings
  TCPIP.INS   start-up file (defines the commands)
  SOCK.H TCPMSG.H NETCFG.H CMDLIN.H SOCK.OBJ RESOLV.OBJ ETHIO.OBJ
  CMDLIN.OBJ  for programs
  *.C *.MAC  sources

Start at boot: add  $ @SY:[0,91]TCPIP.INS  to [0,1]START.COM.

Changes in V1.01: TDIR lists the MiSTer tape library's folders (TDIR,
TDIR RSTS, TDIR RSTS/V9.6/*.TAP); TLOAD takes folder/name, and a name
alone is found in whichever folder has it. Both need MiSTer_pdp2011 with
folder support.

V1.00 (since V0.3): IFCONFIG (interface, DHCP lease and statistics);
TLOAD/TDIR (the MiSTer tape library); NETDATE (date and time from a
network time server) and the monitor's set-time fix in XEPAT; PING and
NETDATE follow the line clock's rate (50 or 60 Hz); commands read the
whole line and take RSTS-style qualifiers; CURL's headers-only option is
-h (it was -I, which DCL's upper-casing made the same as -i); CURL and NC
take RSTS-style qualifiers (/OUTPUT=, /HEAD, /LISTEN=, ...).

V0.3: TCP and NC; UDP and DNS for programs, HOST, CURL; the
socket library; TFTP moved into its own job; the DEUNA receive crash fix.

Known limits: no IP fragments; no TLS on RSTS itself (use the proxy);
TCPIPD serves up to 4 TCP connections at a time; SIMH's NAT ignores the
TCP MSS we advertise, so the monitor fix matters there too.
